WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:04.669
- [Narrator] These are sea turtle eggs,

00:00:04.669 --> 00:00:07.731
and there are two ways this
situation can play out.

00:00:07.731 --> 00:00:08.564
There's either:

00:00:08.564 --> 00:00:10.693
♫ Hallelujah

00:00:10.693 --> 00:00:11.526
Or there's:

00:00:11.526 --> 00:00:13.901
(dramatic orchestral music)

00:00:13.901 --> 00:00:15.528
So, how can we make sure this:

00:00:15.528 --> 00:00:17.538
♫ Hallelujah

00:00:17.538 --> 00:00:18.955
doesn't end up like this?

00:00:18.955 --> 00:00:21.338
(dramatic orchestral music)

00:00:21.338 --> 00:00:23.114
Well, take a look again.

00:00:23.114 --> 00:00:25.685
These aren't your
ordinary sea turtle eggs.

00:00:25.685 --> 00:00:27.725
These eggs could one day take down

00:00:27.725 --> 00:00:29.975
an entire poaching network.

00:00:32.797 --> 00:00:35.680
The story of these eggs
starts in Ann Arbor, Michigan.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:37.487
- Hi, I'm Kim Williams-Guillen,

00:00:37.487 --> 00:00:40.203
and I am a conservation ecologist.

00:00:40.203 --> 00:00:42.906
- [Narrator] Kim does research
on a wide variety of animals,

00:00:42.906 --> 00:00:44.706
but we're here to talk about endangered

00:00:44.706 --> 00:00:46.396
and threatened sea turtles.

00:00:46.396 --> 00:00:48.534
- With sea turtles in Central America,

00:00:48.534 --> 00:00:51.706
there's an issue of poaching of the nests

00:00:51.706 --> 00:00:53.935
for consumption of the eggs.

00:00:53.935 --> 00:00:55.415
- [Narrator] And so,
her team has developed

00:00:55.415 --> 00:00:57.786
a unique solution to
gather more information

00:00:57.786 --> 00:01:01.953
about the poaching trade:
fake eggs with GPS trackers.

00:01:04.055 --> 00:01:08.294
- We can hide these fake turtle
eggs in sea turtle nests.

00:01:08.294 --> 00:01:11.292
Our hope is that these
eggs will get poached along

00:01:11.292 --> 00:01:15.404
with the others and transmit
information on their locations.

00:01:15.404 --> 00:01:19.866
Where is it that they go after
they're taken from the beach?

00:01:19.866 --> 00:01:20.962
- [Narrator] But for this to work,

00:01:20.962 --> 00:01:23.234
the fake eggs have to be convincing.

00:01:23.234 --> 00:01:25.548
- They have to have the right feel

00:01:25.548 --> 00:01:27.179
because sea turtle eggs are kind of

00:01:27.179 --> 00:01:28.459
a little soft and squishy.

00:01:28.459 --> 00:01:29.939
They're not like chicken eggs.

00:01:29.939 --> 00:01:32.314
We've been using a 3D printer

00:01:32.314 --> 00:01:34.560
and a kind of flexible plastic,

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:37.280
but they still don't look quite right.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:38.661
- [Narrator] And this
is when our story moves

00:01:38.661 --> 00:01:40.672
to Los Angeles.

00:01:40.672 --> 00:01:42.468
- Hello, I'm Lauren.

00:01:42.468 --> 00:01:44.191
- [Narrator] And Lauren
works in Hollywood.

00:01:44.191 --> 00:01:47.210
- My day-to-day job is I work
on set as a makeup artist,

00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:50.170
which could be beauty makeup, prosthetics,

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:53.136
creature design, wigs, blood.

00:01:53.136 --> 00:01:54.456
- [Narrator] So it made sense for Lauren

00:01:54.456 --> 00:01:56.695
to help Kim make fake eggs.

00:01:56.695 --> 00:02:00.688
- We needed these eggs to look
realistic, to feel realistic,

00:02:00.688 --> 00:02:03.119
and so that's kind of where I've come in.

00:02:03.119 --> 00:02:04.412
It was like the best way that

00:02:04.412 --> 00:02:07.261
I could use my skills making prosthetics,

00:02:07.261 --> 00:02:10.683
but to actually like help
a whole species of turtles.

00:02:10.683 --> 00:02:12.144
It's like, it’s a dream job.

00:02:12.144 --> 00:02:13.612
- [Narrator] After months of revisions,

00:02:13.612 --> 00:02:17.403
it's time to put these eggs
to the test in Costa Rica.

00:02:17.403 --> 00:02:18.403
- Hi again.

00:02:18.403 --> 00:02:19.843
- [Narrator] You remember Kim.

00:02:19.843 --> 00:02:21.792
She's here to deploy the eggs herself

00:02:21.792 --> 00:02:24.013
on a protected conservation area.

00:02:24.013 --> 00:02:25.824
- This is a place that's very special

00:02:25.824 --> 00:02:29.407
because it's a very
important nesting site.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:35.573
Tonight, a sea turtle will
come and I can test out putting

00:02:35.573 --> 00:02:39.392
the false sea turtle eggs in the nest.

00:02:39.392 --> 00:02:41.861
- [Narrator] Not only will
this project provide Kim's team

00:02:41.861 --> 00:02:44.067
with valuable information on poaching,

00:02:44.067 --> 00:02:47.455
she and Lauren hope it will
inspire others as well.

00:02:47.455 --> 00:02:49.736
- Wildlife poaching can be abstract

00:02:49.736 --> 00:02:52.445
or too depressing to even think about.

00:02:52.445 --> 00:02:54.533
Something about this project kind

00:02:54.533 --> 00:02:56.725
of captures people's attention.

00:02:56.725 --> 00:02:59.595
Here's this very simple,
unique solution that,

00:02:59.595 --> 00:03:02.925
I think, then serves
as a jumping off point

00:03:02.925 --> 00:03:07.635
for people's imaginations to
learn more about this trade.

00:03:07.635 --> 00:03:10.024
- All of us working on this
project are just doing it

00:03:10.024 --> 00:03:13.331
in our weird little nooks
and wherever we can,

00:03:13.331 --> 00:03:16.973
and it just goes to show
there's so many little ways

00:03:16.973 --> 00:03:18.503
that everyone can help.

00:03:18.503 --> 00:03:20.533
You don't have to be
making fake turtle eggs

00:03:20.533 --> 00:03:21.387
to make a difference.

00:03:21.387 --> 00:03:24.887
You can make a difference in your own way.

